## Docker Compose (single host)

For staging or small production:

```bash
# 1. Configure
cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
# Edit backend/.env with production values (see ENV_VARS.md)

# 2. Build + start
docker compose up -d --build

# 3. Apply migrations
docker compose exec app uv run alembic upgrade head


# 4. Verify
curl http://localhost:{{ backend_port }}/api/v1/health
{% if use_frontend %}# Frontend: http://localhost:{{ frontend_port }}{% endif %}
```

{% if use_frontend %}
### Serving from a host that isn't localhost

The frontend is a static build: the API address the browser uses is fixed when the image is
built, from `VITE_API_URL` (default `https://api.DOMAIN` behind Traefik). Opening the app from
another address means setting it and rebuilding:

```bash
# In backend/.env (read by docker-compose.prod.yml)
VITE_API_URL=https://api.example.com   # an address the BROWSER can reach
docker compose --env-file backend/.env -f docker-compose.prod.yml build --no-cache frontend
docker compose --env-file backend/.env -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d frontend
```

The API must allow the frontend's origin: set `CORS_ORIGINS` and `FRONTEND_URL` in
`backend/.env` to the address users open.
{% endif %}
### Reverse proxy

`docker-compose.prod.yml` runs Traefik: `DOMAIN` is routed to the frontend and `api.DOMAIN` to
the API (WebSocket included), with certificates from Let's Encrypt for `ACME_EMAIL`. Point both
DNS records at the host and keep ports 80 and 443 free.

`DOMAIN`, `ACME_EMAIL`, `TRAEFIK_DASHBOARD_AUTH` (`htpasswd -nB admin`, every `$` doubled),
`POSTGRES_PASSWORD` and `REDIS_PASSWORD` are required: Compose stops with a message naming the
missing one instead of starting with a stand-in. Traefik and the API share a network with a
fixed subnet (`PROXY_NETWORK_SUBNET`, default `{{ proxy_network_subnet }}`), and the API trusts
the client address and scheme of `X-Forwarded-*` headers from that subnet only
(`FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS`). Change the subnet if it overlaps a network of the host.

The Valkey password reaches the container as a Compose secret (a file under `/run/secrets`)
and Valkey reads it from a configuration file written at start, so it is not on a command
line (`ps`, `docker inspect`). The API and the workers still receive it, with the other
settings, as environment variables, which `docker inspect` shows to anyone allowed to use the
Docker daemon: on a shared host, use a secret manager or Docker secrets for the application
settings as well.
{% endif %}

### Proxies, gateways and request bodies

`DELETE /api/v1/users/me` carries the current password in its body. Some proxies, gateways and
web application firewalls drop the body of a `DELETE` request. The route then receives no
password and refuses the deletion (`400`): it fails closed, but users cannot delete their
accounts. Check that every intermediary in front of the API forwards `DELETE` bodies.

